      <h1><span class="chrome-text">How do you know your simulation is correct?</span><br><span class="white">Six solvers, one horn, and ±0.0075 dB</span></h1>
      <div class="post-byline"><span><strong>Dom Belgiovane, PhD</strong></span><span>October 2026</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="post container-narrow">
    <div class="measure">
      <p>How do you know your simulation is correct?</p>
      <p>It is an uncomfortable question, because the usual answer is "it converged." But converged on what? A solver can converge cleanly on the wrong answer and give you no warning at all. I learned that firsthand on a project where we needed simulation to be not just good, but extremely accurate: hundredths of a dB on the gain of a standard gain horn.</p>
      <p>This post walks through that work, published at the 2022 AMTA Symposium as "Antenna Gain Calibration with Improved Accuracy Modeling of Pyramidal Standard Gain Horns," with Andrea Giacomini, Francesco Saccardi and Lars J. Foged of Microwave Vision Italy, and Justin Dobbins and me, then at Raytheon Technologies.</p>

      <h2>Why a horn needs better accuracy</h2>
      <p>Most antenna gain measurements use the gain transfer method: you measure your antenna against a reference whose gain you already know. That reference is very often a pyramidal standard gain horn (SGH), and its gain usually comes from the NRL curves based on the Schelkunoff and Slayton formulation. Because of approximations in that formulation, Slayton assessed an uncertainty of ±0.3 dB for typical SGHs operating above 2.6 GHz, back in 1954. That number is still in use, and it is often one of the largest terms in a range's gain uncertainty budget.</p>
      <p>Many studies have tried to improve on Slayton's expressions, but none has seen wide adoption. Our question was whether commercially available full-wave tools could predict the on-axis gain of a real, off-the-shelf horn well enough to shrink that term. The first step is proving the tools agree with each other.</p>

      <h2>One horn, one geometry</h2>
      <p>We modeled a commercial off-the-shelf X-band horn, the SGH820, with interior surfaces compliant to the NRL dimensions. Every tool used the same nominal interior geometry: a = 194.4 mm, b = 144.0 mm, le = 320.0 mm and lh = 342.5 mm, with a 2 mm wall thickness at the aperture edges. The horn was fed from a WR-90 transition section (22.86 mm by 10.16 mm, 33.8 mm long), with a waveport at its end.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:560px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/008-db-calibrated-horn/images/fig3-sgh820-interior-dimensions.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/008-db-calibrated-horn/images/fig3-sgh820-interior-dimensions.jpg" width="560" height="365" alt="Interior dimensions of the SGH820 (Fig. 3 of the 2022 paper)"></picture></div>
