    <div class="measure">
      <p>To remove geometry error, the model was split and mirrored across the principal planes so it was exactly symmetric, and symmetry planes were used wherever a tool allowed, since a symmetric mesh can suppress very small meshing errors. The horn was modeled as aluminum, with the same properties in every tool (σe = 3.8 × 10^7 S/m, μr = 1.000021, from the HFSS materials library).</p>

      <h2>Six solver runs, four numerical methods</h2>
      <p>This was not one method run three times. We used:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>FEKO:</strong> direct Method of Moments. We chose direct MoM over MLFMM because MLFMM can cost accuracy if the residual is not set low enough.</li>
        <li><strong>HFSS:</strong> the finite element method, with a PML-bounded air box.</li>
        <li><strong>CST Studio Suite:</strong> four ways, finite integration technique (FIT), transmission line method (TLM), MoM, and FEM.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Every run covered 8.2 to 12.4 GHz in 25 MHz steps, 169 frequencies, fine enough to capture the ripple in the boresight directivity. That ripple comes from edge diffraction at the aperture, and it is exactly what the classic Schelkunoff solution leaves out.</p>
      <p>The methodology followed IEEE Std 1597.1 and 1597.2 for validating computational electromagnetics: iterative mesh refinement as self-referencing, and multiple independent codes as external referencing.</p>

      <h2>The mesher that converged on the wrong answer</h2>
      <p>Here is the lesson I would put at the top of any simulation checklist.</p>
      <p>HFSS has an excellent adaptive mesher. It refines the mesh until the S-parameters stop changing. But a horn is a well-matched antenna: very little energy reflects back to the port. So the S-parameters settle long before the fields at the aperture edges are resolved. Our initial HFSS model converged on an incorrect solution because the adaptive mesher never captured the diffraction at the edge of the horn.</p>
      <p>The fix was a stricter manual mesh:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>λ/20 at 12 GHz (1.25 mm) on the aperture edges</li>
        <li>λ/8 at 12 GHz (3.125 mm) on the interior and exterior flare surfaces</li>
        <li>1 mm at the waveport feed</li>
        <li>second-order basis functions</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Only with that in place did HFSS agree with the other tools. The broader point: converge on the quantity you report. If you care about gain, an S-parameter convergence criterion is not proof of anything.</p>
      <p>The other tools were pushed too. In FEKO, going from standard to fine mesh, and adding higher-order basis functions, made no appreciable difference. CST FIT was adapted down to a λ/100 mesh step and TLM used λ/80. CST FEM started at λ/3 and refined adaptively with higher solver order. HFSS uses an air box and MoM does not, so their agreement is also evidence that reflections from the PML boundaries were negligible.</p>

      <h2>Compare against the mean, not a favorite</h2>
      <p>When six curves lie on top of each other, an overlay tells you very little. All of the CEM results agree extraordinarily well, and the visible story is how they differ from the NRL curve, which overestimates directivity especially at the higher frequencies.</p>
    </div>

