    <div class="measure">
      <p>To see the differences between solvers, we averaged all the simulated directivity curves and subtracted that mean from each one. Nobody's tool is the reference; the ensemble is.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:744px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/008-db-calibrated-horn/images/fig5-difference-from-mean.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/008-db-calibrated-horn/images/fig5-difference-from-mean.jpg" width="744" height="467" alt="Each solver's directivity minus the mean of all solvers (Fig. 5)"></picture></div>
      <figcaption>Each solver's directivity minus the mean of all solvers (Fig. 5)</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>This view exposes bias you cannot see in an overlay. FEKO, for example, sits consistently on the positive side of the mean. Even so, the solutions land within ±0.0075 dB of the average curve, and the simulations agree predominantly to three decimal places across the band. Using the feature selective validation (FSV) method, the results rated "Excellent" on the Validation Rating Scale.</p>

      <h2>Directivity, gain, and what is left</h2>
      <p>With the aluminum model, the difference between directivity and IEEE gain gives conductor loss: only 0.006 to 0.012 dB. The difference between directivity and realized gain showed a best-case mismatch loss of 0.022 dB with an idealized waveport, which would likely grow with a real coax-to-waveguide adapter.</p>
      <p>We also wrote down the error budget, unknowns included:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Differences in code implementation: ±0.0075 dB</li>
        <li>Gain calculation: -0.012 dB</li>
        <li>Higher-order waveguide modes, numerical precision, air box reflections, mesh noise: negligible</li>
        <li>Coax-to-waveguide feed: measurable, not yet modeled</li>
        <li>Physical versus model dimensions: unknown</li>
        <li>Exterior interacting scatterers: +0.070 dB (from earlier published work on absorber collars)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Listing "unknown" is not a weakness. It tells you exactly where the next piece of work has to go. The real SGH820 has edge tapering at the aperture that the model did not include, no horn is perfectly symmetric, and manufacturing tolerances still had to be quantified with a dedicated sensitivity analysis.</p>

      <h2>What this answers, and what it does not</h2>
      <p>Back to the opening question. This paper does not prove the simulation matches the hardware. What it proves is narrower and still important: for a well-defined geometry, four numerical methods in three commercial tools land on the same answer to better than a hundredth of a dB, provided you mesh for the physics that matters. When simulation and measurement disagree by a tenth of a dB, the solver is not the first place to look.</p>
      <p>Paper (IEEE Xplore): <a href="https://doi.org/10.23919/AMTA55213.2022.9954972" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://doi.org/10.23919/AMTA55213.2022.9954972</a></p>

      <h2>Future work</h2>
      <p>Agreement between solvers only proves the solvers agree. The paper closed by listing what still had to be done before the simulated gain could stand in for a calibration:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Manufacturing tolerances.</strong> Manufacturing will always add uncertainty to the horn's dimensions, so the effect of those tolerances needs its own sensitivity analysis.</li>
        <li><strong>Measured geometry.</strong> Metrology scans of the manufactured parts could quantify the real dimensions, and the scanned geometry could be modeled directly in the CEM tools.</li>
        <li><strong>Measurement as the external reference.</strong> Following the IEEE 1597.1 validation flow, the horn needs to be measured and compared against the simulations, with the agreement scored using the feature selective validation (FSV) method and its rating scale.</li>
        <li><strong>Real-world scatterers.</strong> Interactions with things around the horn, such as absorber collars, mechanical positioners, and horn flanges, need to be accounted for to tighten the gain error terms.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In the next post, we hold the model up against measurement of a real horn and see what it takes to close the gap.</p>
